{"connection_id":"BC-d31b89599b4c4bcd025c","ref":"WLC:Dan.4.30","status":"rejected","reason":"WLC 4:30 narrates Nebuchadnezzar's abasement, not the boast; the boast is WLC 4:27. The cited verse cannot evidence the proposed speech.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.4.30"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-e92316dd4de7c0ad0742","ref":"WLC:Dan.4.31","status":"rejected","reason":"WLC 4:31 is the king lifting his eyes and blessing the Most High; the interrupting heavenly voice is WLC 4:28.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.4.31"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-5aa993d106fc7fd34584","ref":"WLC:Dan.4.34","status":"rejected","reason":"WLC 4:34 praises the King of heaven and his humbling of the proud but does not contain the proposed Aramaic Most High title; that form occurs in 4:31.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.4.34"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-c7354ee0a965aeabfe3d","ref":"WLC:Dan.4.34","status":"accepted","reason":"The humbled king explicitly praises the King of heaven and says he can humble the proud; this is retained alongside other royal claims.","paragraphs":[42],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.14.14","WLC:Isa.14.15","WLC:Ezek.28.2","WLC:Dan.4.34"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-007"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-749826993518481ca46a","ref":"WLC:Dan.5.20","status":"rejected","reason":"The deposed proud king adds another royal reversal, but Daniel 4:34 already gives its outcome in the king's own praise, which is the relevant name/high contrast.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.5.20"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-c27460157ab183cd1548","ref":"WLC:Dan.5.23","status":"rejected","reason":"Belshazzar's defiance of heaven's Lord is explicit but is another royal example of the divine-claim pattern already shown by Isaiah 14:14 and Ezekiel 28:2.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.5.23"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-8d7cae3cdfb0bcb153bc","ref":"WLC:Deut.12.11","status":"rejected","reason":"This concerns Israel's single chosen sacrificial site, whereas the cited Qur'an 24:36 speaks of houses in the plural; 1 Kings 8:29 gives the name/prayer relation without implying identical sanctuary law.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.12.11"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-06424a930e3cf2a76610","ref":"WLC:Deut.12.5","status":"rejected","reason":"The place chosen for the divine name belongs to Deuteronomy's centralized sanctuary rule; paragraph 42's plural houses cannot be treated as the same institution.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.12.5"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-fe114f2d14a738a68cd4","ref":"WLC:Deut.32.3","status":"accepted","reason":"Moses explicitly proclaims the name and asks hearers to ascribe greatness to God.","paragraphs":[40],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.32.3","WLC:Neh.9.5"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-aeb3485fb4de8c9294d5","ref":"WLC:Deut.4.24","status":"rejected","reason":"The Lord as consuming fire describes holiness and covenant jealousy, not the fire entered by the wicked at 87:12; Exodus 24:17 better distinguishes divine appearance from punishment.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.4.24"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-743602b10c3f8c8725d8","ref":"WLC:Deut.8.17","status":"rejected","reason":"The self-crediting of gained wealth is a useful economic warning but does not claim divine or supreme lordship; Isaiah 14:14 and Ezekiel 28:2 fit Pharaoh's usurpation exactly.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.8.17"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-2c401f79efb39e011b54","ref":"WLC:Deut.8.17","status":"rejected","reason":"The same wealth/self-credit warning is more specific to prosperity than to the page's high divine title and near-life valuation; Matthew 6:19-21 handles chosen earthly goods.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.8.17"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-4e2ee080713c470eca64","ref":"WLC:Exod.15.1","status":"rejected","reason":"Moses sings of YHWH's triumph over horse and rider, a shared Pharaoh setting, but the verse lacks the name/high-title antithesis that paragraph 42 highlights; 15:11 supplies holiness.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.1"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-09d4b86dbe85b2af0d75","ref":"WLC:Exod.15.11","status":"accepted","reason":"The sea song explicitly joins incomparable divine holiness, majesty and praise, giving positive content to tasbih.","paragraphs":[40],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.11","WLC:Isa.6.3"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3e3645a7f1994cbadc4b","ref":"WLC:Exod.15.11","status":"accepted","reason":"The repeated proposal's majestic holiness phrase is quoted in paragraph 40's note.","paragraphs":[40],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.11","WLC:Isa.6.3"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-e4e765e808b7f21a996b","ref":"WLC:Exod.15.2","status":"rejected","reason":"Moses praises and exalts his God after the sea rescue, but the verse does not name the divine name; Deuteronomy 32:3 more closely joins name and greatness.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.2"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3601d040a4dc30458369","ref":"WLC:Exod.18.11","status":"rejected","reason":"Jethro calls YHWH greater than other gods after Egypt's presumption, but there is no self-bestowed highest title or divine-name formula in this verse.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.18.11"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-2b5a3394ed0a4fb4b198","ref":"WLC:Exod.19.20","status":"rejected","reason":"Moses literally ascends Sinai's summit, but the commentary's taʿal root observation is an Arabic lexical point; physical ascent here adds no comparative reading of the divine name or near life.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.19.20"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-1cc853e92d451ff8c0e6","ref":"WLC:Exod.20.24","status":"accepted","reason":"God promises to bless where he makes his name remembered; this qualifies the house/name prayer image.","paragraphs":[42],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.20.24","WLC:1Kgs.8.29","WLC:1Kgs.8.27"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-44406fb04e748b42c2d0","ref":"WLC:Exod.20.24","status":"accepted","reason":"The remembered name at a worship place is explicitly stated in paragraph 42's spatial note.","paragraphs":[42],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.20.24","WLC:1Kgs.8.29","WLC:1Kgs.8.27"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-212b34cadc188e025e14","ref":"WLC:Exod.24.1","status":"rejected","reason":"'Come up' calls Moses to a real mountain, but this does not establish or illuminate the Arabic etymology of taʿal in paragraph 39; Isaiah 2:3 repeats the same ascent motif.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.24.1"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-fc79d486d9eff3d67d55","ref":"WLC:Exod.24.17","status":"accepted","reason":"At Sinai divine glory visibly resembles devouring fire at the summit; the linked note distinguishes that scene from 87:12's punitive fire.","paragraphs":[40],"evidence":["WLC:Num.6.25","WLC:Exod.24.17"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-004"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-45050b1e85a9197357e8","ref":"WLC:Exod.3.15","status":"accepted","reason":"Hebrew zikri means my memorial and is paired with God's enduring name, giving a concrete z-k-r comparison.","paragraphs":[39],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.3.15"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-SYD-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-47a290d8d54f765759b4","ref":"WLC:Exod.3.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse explicitly coordinates God's name and memorial through generations.","paragraphs":[39],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.3.15"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-SYD-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-1ad83390424cc5cfa183","ref":"WLC:Exod.3.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The pointed zikri form is present and is compared cautiously with Arabic dhikr, without borrowing claim.","paragraphs":[39],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.3.15"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-SYD-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-69abd3fcafe3ce732d8c","ref":"WLC:Exod.3.15","status":"accepted","reason":"WLC has the inflected shemi, not an isolated shem; the cautious name-cognate comparison is retained without inferring the Arabic height etymology.","paragraphs":[39],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.3.15"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-SYD-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-96d31280bf354cc14611","ref":"WLC:Exod.3.6","status":"rejected","reason":"Moses fears looking at God at the bush; Qur'an 20:67-68 in paragraph 42 is fear of the magicians' display, a separate episode and cause.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.3.6"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-4bdb020a3eff517effdd","ref":"WLC:Exod.33.20","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse forbids seeing God's face and living, but says nothing of its light; Numbers 6:25 provides the positive radiant-face usage closer to paragraph 40's gloss.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.33.20"],"annotations":[]}

{"connection_id":"BC-dde2d4c55db51994e56e","ref":"WLC:Exod.9.17","status":"rejected","reason":"Pharaoh is still obstructing God's people, but the Hebrew form here concerns his conduct toward them rather than a claim to God's supreme title; 9:16 gives the name contrast.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.9.17"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-82a44769f8407ac3759c","ref":"WLC:Ezek.28.17","status":"rejected","reason":"The ruler's proud heart and fall continue Ezekiel's oracle, but 28:2 states his explicit 'I am a god' claim and human correction more closely beside Pharaoh.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.28.17"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-9d7b99e8779e23b0969a","ref":"WLC:Ezek.28.18","status":"rejected","reason":"Fire consumes the Tyrian ruler, but paragraph 42 focuses the claim to divine rank, which 28:2 states; paragraph 40's fire mention is not a royal-judgment reading.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.28.18"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-85dde85c5b474950e1b3","ref":"WLC:Ezek.28.2","status":"accepted","reason":"The Tyrian ruler says 'I am a god' and is answered 'you are a human,' a precise comparator for appropriated lordship.","paragraphs":[42],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.14.14","WLC:Isa.14.15","WLC:Ezek.28.2","WLC:Dan.4.34"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-007"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-62c9fa0c46057617f8d6","ref":"WLC:Ezek.36.23","status":"rejected","reason":"God himself sanctifies the great name after its profanation among nations, a restoration oracle distinct from the human command to praise the name in this section.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.36.23"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-193f11eed642679776a7","ref":"WLC:Gen.11.4","status":"accepted","reason":"The tower builders' self-made name and heaven-reaching structure directly pair attempted height with human name-making.","paragraphs":[42],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.11.4","WLC:Gen.12.2"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-008"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-fe9520efd36cadfb751b","ref":"WLC:Gen.11.4","status":"rejected","reason":"Hebrew shem is a possible lexical comparison, but the cognate point is already made with the divine name and memorial in Exodus 3:15; the Babel annotation uses this verse for its distinct self-making scene.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.11.4"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-7ef31d22b5bd09a4a7b5","ref":"WLC:Gen.11.4","status":"accepted","reason":"Self-made name and heaven-reaching tower provide a human counter-scene to divine-name glorification.","paragraphs":[42],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.11.4","WLC:Gen.12.2"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-008"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-f324598577b3e98ccad6","ref":"WLC:Gen.11.8","status":"rejected","reason":"Scattering and stopping the city follow the tower episode, but this verse no longer mentions the self-made name or heavenward building; 11:4 contains the section's specific point.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.11.8"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3606a28eabf8a2eebc1f","ref":"WLC:Gen.12.2","status":"accepted","reason":"God promises to make Abram's name great, directly contrasting Babel's self-made name.","paragraphs":[42],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.11.4","WLC:Gen.12.2"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-008"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-0068e841020a0473f426","ref":"WLC:Gen.14.19","status":"accepted","reason":"Melchizedek's blessing explicitly applies Most High to the creator of heaven and earth.","paragraphs":[39],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.14.19"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-SYD-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-2f3af48df5b3f1b0e8a5","ref":"WLC:Gen.14.19","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew Elyon title is cautiously compared with Arabic al-Aʿla as a Semitic height-title counterpart, without an influence claim.","paragraphs":[39],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.14.19"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-SYD-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-727e20d4bdf31d7ee5d0","ref":"WLC:Gen.14.20","status":"rejected","reason":"This repeats blessing of El Elyon in the immediately preceding verse; Genesis 14:19 adds the creator identification needed for paragraph 39's title comparison.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.14.20"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-ac93ac15a1001d68a6e4","ref":"WLC:Hag.1.8","status":"rejected","reason":"The command is to go up for wood and build a house; it does not mention God's name or name-remembrance, and WLC ketiv/qere distinguish the final glorification form.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Hag.1.8"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-5d9ce4307d202625b30a","ref":"WLC:Isa.12.4","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse commands invocation and proclamation that God's name is exalted, strengthening the name/high formula in paragraph 39.","paragraphs":[39],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.7.18","WLC:Ps.92.2","WLC:Ps.135.1","WLC:Isa.12.4"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3097a2d724bb11fef681","ref":"WLC:Isa.14.12","status":"rejected","reason":"The fallen morning-star simile is part of the king taunt, but the commentary's horizon silhouette is a lexical illustration, not this astronomical fall; 14:14-15 states the actual high/low claim.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.14.12"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-113fc5fe73f45e19c92a","ref":"WLC:Isa.14.14","status":"accepted","reason":"The Babylon king's boast explicitly aspires to likeness with Elyon, a close comparator to Pharaoh's appropriated supreme rank.","paragraphs":[42],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.14.14","WLC:Isa.14.15","WLC:Isa.2.11","WLC:Ezek.28.2","WLC:Dan.4.34"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-007"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-7c73ab12b94650e65998","ref":"WLC:Isa.14.14","status":"accepted","reason":"The second proposal identifies the same explicit Most High likeness boast, quoted in paragraph 42.","paragraphs":[42],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.14.14","WLC:Isa.14.15","WLC:Isa.2.11","WLC:Ezek.28.2","WLC:Dan.4.34"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-007"]}

{"connection_id":"BC-f2ff269cca7d02053de1","ref":"WLC:Isa.42.8","status":"rejected","reason":"God says his glory goes to no other and no idols; paragraph 42 concerns a human ruler's claimed divine rank, addressed more explicitly by Isaiah 14:14 and Ezekiel 28:2.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.42.8"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-b2a4e795c64b06be1b3a","ref":"WLC:Isa.56.5","status":"rejected","reason":"God gives outsiders an everlasting name, but the section contrasts self-made with divinely bestowed renown through adjacent Genesis 11:4 and 12:2, which are structurally closer.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.56.5"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-643e84eadf335cb316ce","ref":"WLC:Isa.57.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The high and holy God dwells with the contrite lowly, preventing a spatial or contemptuous reading of the high/low axis.","paragraphs":[41],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.57.15"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-005"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-e204cb59a01a3ee52f0a","ref":"WLC:Isa.57.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse also names the high one's holiness; both title/name and dwelling-with-lowly are retained in paragraph 41.","paragraphs":[41],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.57.15"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-005"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-c8896078e56cb04d7f10","ref":"WLC:Isa.6.3","status":"accepted","reason":"The seraphic triple holiness and earth-filling glory illustrate the sacred majesty side of praise.","paragraphs":[40],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.11","WLC:Isa.6.3","WLC:Lev.22.32"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-f37a524042333b31d190","ref":"WLC:Isa.66.24","status":"rejected","reason":"The unquenched fire is for the corpses of rebels; it does not describe 87:13's neither-dying-nor-living condition, which this section does not interpret.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.66.24"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-6f0b4ee24b56377a37df","ref":"WLC:Isa.66.24","status":"rejected","reason":"This proposal repeats the unquenched-fire comparison, but the page only invokes 87:12 through the k-b-r root, so the punishment detail would be misplaced.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.66.24"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-a08047ffbf20e9301fd5","ref":"WLC:Job.1.21","status":"rejected","reason":"Job blesses the name after loss; the commendable endurance does not bear on the high title or near-life choice argued in these four paragraphs.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Job.1.21"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-36e24b835ab3c5811936","ref":"WLC:Job.36.5","status":"accepted","reason":"Hebrew kabbir calls God mighty and provides a cautious Semitic comparison for the Arabic greatness root discussed at paragraph 40.","paragraphs":[40],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.32.3","WLC:Neh.9.5","WLC:Job.36.5"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-93fa4b2a12f0440e4c29","ref":"WLC:Job.36.5","status":"accepted","reason":"The attested kabbir word is explicitly mentioned as a comparison, without asserting borrowing or identity of meanings.","paragraphs":[40],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.32.3","WLC:Neh.9.5","WLC:Job.36.5"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-65d6e6f9a8498a2e02e9","ref":"WLC:Lev.22.32","status":"accepted","reason":"The holiness of God's name is an ethical command against profanation, supplementing the praise formula.","paragraphs":[40],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.15.11","WLC:Isa.6.3","WLC:Lev.22.32"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-716a0c588054f9a5e269","ref":"WLC:Mal.1.11","status":"accepted","reason":"The great divine name among nations and offerings tie name-honor to worship, expressed in paragraph 42.","paragraphs":[42],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.20.24","WLC:Mal.1.11","WLC:1Kgs.8.29","WLC:1Kgs.8.27"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-e35f65397bda8b09657d","ref":"WLC:Mal.1.11","status":"accepted","reason":"The same great-name and offering point is stated in the linked worship-place note.","paragraphs":[42],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.20.24","WLC:Mal.1.11","WLC:1Kgs.8.29","WLC:1Kgs.8.27"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-009"]}

